l equivalence class.\nA ﬁlling of a Young diagram is semistandard if the entries increase\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSupport Equalities of Ribbon Schur Functions (https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~reiner/REU/GaetzHardtSridharTran_prob1_2017.pdf)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; We have proven that the condition in Theorem 2.1 is necessary for a ribbon to\nhave full equivalence class, and have just conjectured it to be suﬃcient. ... In this section, we support Conjecture 2.4 by proving that the condition it mentions ... Ribbons With 3 Rows ... state the lemmas that we need for the proof of Theorem 3.4 and a deﬁnition needed\nWe have proven that the condition in Theorem 2.1 is necessary for a ribbon to\nhave full equivalence class, and have just conjectured it to be suﬃcient. We now\nshow that this condition is in fact suﬃcient for m = 3 and m = 4. We have also\nveriﬁed by computation that this condition is suﬃcient for m = 5, m = 6, and m = 7\nfor certain n (see Appendix for sample code).\n3\nGeneral Results Applied to Small Cases\nIn this section, we support Conjecture 2.4 by proving that the condition it mentions\nis both necessary and suﬃcient for m = 3 and m = 4.\n3.1\nRibbons With 3 Rows\nLet us ﬁrst consider what the condition from Conjecture 2.4 is in the m = 3 case.\nThis condition requires that 1 ≤j ≤m −2 = 1, meaning that we only need to\nconsider j = 1. Then by Remark 2.2, Nj = αj = α1. The conjectured necessary and\nsuﬃcient condition from Conjecture 2.4 therefore amounts to\n3\nX\nN1 = α1 <\ni=2\nαi −(m −j −2) = α2 + α3 −(3 −1 −2) = α2 + α3\n,\nAs we see, Conjecture 2.4 would imply that when m = 3, having row lengths\nwhich satisfy the strict triangle inequality is both necessary and suﬃcient for having\nfull equivalence class. Before proving in Theorem 3.4 that this is in fact the case, we\nstate the lemmas that we need for the proof of Theorem 3.4 and a deﬁnition needed\nfor the lemmas. Since the proofs of these lemmas are long and technical, we will\ndefer their proofs until later.\nDeﬁnition 3.1. In a ribbon α = (α1, α2, . . . , αm), a row j is considered to be long\nif 1 ≤j < m and αj > Pj−1\ni=1 αi −2(j −2), or if j = m and αj ≥Pj−1\ni=1 αi −2(j −2).\nLemma 3.2. Let αj be the longest length of a row in the connected ribbon α. Then\nno long rows occur beneath the ﬁrst occurrence of a row of length αj.\nLemma 3.3. Let α = (α1, α2, . . . , αm) be a ribbon. Let j be the largest row index such\nthat row j is long; if α has no long rows, let j = 1. Let ℓ= max(αj + m −2, ⌈n/2⌉)\nif j = 1 or j = m, and let ℓ= max(αj + m −3, ⌈n/2⌉) if 1 < j < m. Then there\nexists a 1-2 LR-ﬁlling with p 1’s if and only if\nℓ≤p ≤n −(m −1).\n20--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nYoung Mathematicians Conference 2018 (https://ymc.math.osu.edu/2018/abstract-sched.php?ID_array=GM10192619)\nciteturn2search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; that two connected ribbons (i.e. connected skew shapes without 2×2 shapes as subdiagrams) ... condition for a connected ribbon α to have full equivalence class. ... separate necessary condition, which we conjecture to be suﬃcient.\nYoung Mathematicians Conference 2018\nThe Ohio State University, August 10-12\nSupport Equalities Among Ribbon Schur Functions\nMarisa R Gaetz\n(mgaetz@mit.edu)\nUniv. of MN - Twin Cities\n[Mentor:Pavlo Pylyavskyy]\nAbstract of Poster Presentation: Schur functions are certain symmetric polynomials that are\nindexed by partitions; they form what is often considered the most important linear basis for\nthe space of symmetric functions and are of particular importance to the ﬁelds of algebraic\ncombinatorics and representation theory. Relatedly, a skew Schur function corresponds to a\npair of partitions λ and µ (which determine a skew shape λ/µ). A skew Schur function sλ/µ\ncan be written as a linear combination of some Schur functions sν1, sν2, . . . , sνn, and the set\n{sν1, sν2, . . . , sνn} is called the Schur support of sλ/µ. A result of P.R.W. McNamara implies\nthat two connected ribbons (i.e. connected skew shapes without 2×2 shapes as subdiagrams)\ncan have the same Schur support only if one is obtained by permuting row lengths of the\nother. We present substantial progress towards classifying when a permutation π ∈Sm of\nrow lengths of a connected ribbon α produces a ribbon απ with the same Schur support as α;\nwhen this occurs for all π ∈Sm, we say that α has full equivalence class. Our main results,\nthe proofs of which incorporate the celebrated Littlewood-Richardson rule, include a suﬃcient\ncondition for a connected ribbon α to have full equivalence class. Additionally, we prove a\nseparate necessary condition, which we conjecture to be suﬃcient.\n[Joint work with Will Hardt and Shruthi Sridhar]\nReceived: July 18, 2018--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSupport Equalities Among Ribbon Schur Functions (https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.03011)\nciteturn2academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.2 years ago; This implies that two ribbons can have the same Schur support only if one is obtained by permuting row lengths of the other. We present substantial progress towards classifying when a permutation $\\pi \\in S_m$ of row lengths of a ribbon $\\alpha$ produces a ribbon $\\alpha_{\\pi}$ with the same Schur support as $\\alpha$; when this occurs for all $\\pi \\in S_m$, we say that $\\alpha$ has \"full equivalence class.\" ... Additionally, we prove a separate necessary condition, which we conjecture to be sufficient.\nTitle: Support Equalities Among Ribbon Schur Functions\nAuthors: Marisa Gaetz, Will Hardt, Shruthi Sridhar\nDate: Sat Sep  9 22:11:55 2017\n\nIn 2007, McNamara proved that two skew shapes can have the same Schur support only if they have the same number of $k\\times \\ell$ rectangles as subdiagrams. This implies that two ribbons can have the same Schur support only if one is obtained by permuting row lengths of the other. We present substantial progress towards classifying when a permutation $\\pi \\in S_m$ of row lengths of a ribbon $\\alpha$ produces a ribbon $\\alpha_{\\pi}$ with the same Schur support as $\\alpha$; when this occurs for all $\\pi \\in S_m$, we say that $\\alpha$ has \"full equivalence class.\" Our main results include a sufficient condition for a ribbon $\\alpha$ to have full equivalence class. Additionally, we prove a separate necessary condition, which we conjecture to be sufficient.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPolynomial Stillman bound conjecture for projective dimension | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/340598/polynomial-stillman-bound-conjecture-for-projective-dimensio)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Polynomial Stillman bound conjecture for projective dimension\n\n# Polynomial Stillman bound conjecture for projective dimension\n\nAbout 8 years old · traced to 2018\n\nLet $R$R be a polynomial ring over a field, and let $I\\subseteq R$I⊆R be an ideal generated by $n$n forms of degree at most $d$d. Fix $d$d, and let $C_d$C d​ be a positive constant depending only on $d$d.\n\nPolynomial Stillman-bound conjecture. For all characteristics, the projective dimension of $R/I$R/I is at worst\n\n$$C_dn^d.$$C d​n d.\n\nThis is presented as a conjectural polynomial Stillman bound. The paper notes that the analogous conjecture for ${}^\\eta\\!A$η A is verified for $d\\leq 3$d≤3, while the Stillman-bound conjecture is not known even for quadrics.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nTigran Ananyan and Melvin Hochster, “Strength conditions, small subalgebras, and Stillman bounds in degree 4”, arXiv:1810.00413 (2020).\n\ncommutative-algebraprojective-dimensionstillman-conjecture\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSemigroup graded Stillman's conjecture (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0021869325000912)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; The classical Stillman's conjecture ensures a bound shared across the entire family of standard graded polynomial rings at the cost of restricting the desired class of ideals I to those with a particular degree sequence. ... The key intuition is to leverage the known Stillman bound for the {\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}${\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}$-graded case by using bounded factorization to guarantee a nice regrading by {\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}${\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}$ for the “if” direction, and to construct an explicit counterexample using only non-bounded factorization for the “only if” direction.\nThe following example demonstrates that allowing the grading group to have infinite descending chains (i.e. the divisibility order is not well-founded) can prevent Stillman bounds on projective dimension.\n\nExample 1.1\n\nLet ${S}_{n}=k[x,y,{z}_{1},\\dots ,{z}_{n}]$ be a {\\mathbb{Q}}_{+}${\\mathbb{Q}}_{+}$-graded polynomial ring with grading given by $\\mathrm{deg}(x)=\\mathrm{deg}(y)=\\mathrm{deg}({z}_{i})=1/n$. The homogeneous ideal${I}_{n}=〈{x}^{n},{y}^{n},{x}^{n-1}{z}_{1}+{x}^{n-2}y{z}_{2}+\\dots +x{y}^{n-2}{z}_{n-1}+{y}^{n-1}{z}_{n}〉$ is generated by 3 degree 1 elements. It is shown in [23] that the projective dimension of ${S}_{n}/{I}_{n}$ is $n+2$. Therefore the family $\\{{S}_{n}\\}$ of {\\mathbb{Q}}_{+}${\\mathbb{Q}}_{+}$-graded polynomial rings cannot have a Stillman bound since we can make n arbitrarily large while {I}_{n}${I}_{n}$ is generated in degree 1. ⋄\n\nOn the other hand, there is an obvious condition on the grading which is sufficient for the existence of a Stillman bound. If there is a sufficiently nice “flattening” map from the grading group to \\mathbb{Z}$\\mathbb{Z}$, we can regrade our family by {\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}${\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}$ and apply Ananyan and Hochster's result, since changing the grading does not change projective dimension. Theorem 1.2\n\nFor any degree sequence d from Λ, there is a number $N(\\mathrm{\\Lambda },\\mathit{d})$ depending only on Λ and d bounding the projective dimension of any ideal with degree sequence bounded by d in any connected Γ-graded polynomial ring with support contained in Λ if and only if Λ has bounded factorization.\n\nAfter fixing any particular polynomial ring S and ideal I, the projective dimension of $S/I$ does not change as one varies the grading. Hilbert's syzygy theorem guarantees that the projective dimension of every ideal in S is bounded by the (hopefully finite) number of variables in S. The classical Stillman's conjecture ensures a bound shared across the entire family of standard graded polynomial rings at the cost of restricting the desired class of ideals I to those with a particular degree sequence. Theorem 1.2 asserts that if you also allow your grading to vary, under mild conditions you must only further fix Λ for Stillman uniformity. Example 1.1 fails to have Stillman bounded projective dimension exactly because the supports of the family fail to lie in a bounded factorization monoid. Letting $\\mathrm{deg}(x)=\\mathrm{deg}(y)=0$ and $\\mathrm{deg}({z}_{i})=d$ in the same example shows that non-connected gradings also fail to have a Stillman bound.\n\nAfter finding the right condition on Λ, the proof of Theorem 1.2 ends up being very short. The key intuition is to leverage the known Stillman bound for the {\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}${\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}$-graded case by using bounded factorization to guarantee a nice regrading by {\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}${\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}$ for the “if” direction, and to construct an explicit counterexample using only non-bounded factorization for the “only if” direction. Even in the $\\mathrm{\\Lambda }={\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}$ case proven by Ananyan and Hochster [1], Theorem 1.2 says something new; the same Stillman bound holds for families of varying {\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}${\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}$-gradings, with the bounds potentially getting tighter for polynomial rings whose support refines {\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}${\\mathbb{Z}}_{+}$. We end by discussing some other applications and further examples in Section 4.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA classification of monotone ribbons with full Schur support with application to the classification of full equivalence classes (https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.04705)\nciteturn2academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.9 years ago; We then conclude that the Gaetz-Hardt-Sridhar necessary condition for a connected ribbon to have full equivalence class is equivalent to the condition for a monotone connected ribbon to have full Schur support. That is, the set of partitions with full equivalence class is a subset of those monotone connected ribbons with full Schur support.\nTitle: A classification of monotone ribbons with full Schur support with application to the classification of full equivalence classes\nAuthors: Olga Azenhas, Ricardo Mamede\nDate: Tue Dec 11 21:44:06 2018\n\nWe consider ribbon shapes, not necessarily connected, whose rows, with at least two boxes in each, are in monotone length order. These ribbons are uniquely defined by a pair of partitions: the row partition consisting of the row lengths in decreasing order, and the overlapping partition whose entries count the total number of columns with two boxes in the successive ribbon shapes obtained by sequentially subtracting the longest row. The support of such ribbon Schur functions, considered as a subposet of the dominance order lattice on partitions, has the row partition as bottom element, and, as top element, the partition whose two parts consist of the total number of columns, and the total number of columns of length two respectively. We give a complete system of linear inequalities in terms of the partition pair defining the aforesaid ribbon shape under which the ribbon Schur function attains all the Schur interval when expanded in the basis of Schur functions. We then conclude that the Gaetz-Hardt-Sridhar necessary condition for a connected ribbon to have full equivalence class is equivalent to the condition for a monotone connected ribbon to have full Schur support. That is, the set of partitions with full equivalence class is a subset of those monotone connected ribbons with full Schur support. M. Gaetz, W. Hardt and S. Sridhar conjectured that the necessary condition is also sufficient which translates now to every monotone connected ribbon with full Schur support has full equivalence class. The main tool of our analysis is the structure of the companion tableau of a ribbon Littlewood-Richardson (LR) tableau detected by the descent set defined by the composition whose parts are the ribbon row lengths.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch (Igor Pak Home Page) (https://www.math.ucla.edu/~pak/papers/research.htm)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; The results are extended to more general class of regions. ... Tile invariants were first introduced in [Pak], where a full basis of invariants of ribbon tiles was conjectured. Here we present a complete proof of the conjecture, which works by associating ribbon tiles with a certain polygon in the complex plane, and deriving invariants from the signed area of this polygon.\n\n* * *\n\n  * (with Cris Moore) Ribbon tile invariants from signed area, Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Ser. A, vol. 98 (2002), 1-16.\n\nRibbon tiles are polyominoes consisting of n squares laid out in a path, each step of which goes north or east. Tile invariants were first introduced in [Pak], where a full basis of invariants of ribbon tiles was conjectured. Here we present a complete proof of the conjecture, which works by associating ribbon tiles with a certain polygon in the complex plane, and deriving invariants from the signed area of this polygon.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\n\n* * *\n\n  * (with Roman Muchnik) On tilings by ribbon tetrominoes, Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Ser. A, vol. 88 (1999), 188-193.\n\nWe resolve a problem posed in our previous paper by extending the set of regions to all simply connected regions in the case n=4. Conway-Lagarias type technique is employed.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\n\n* * *\n\n  * Ribbon tile invariants, Trans. AMS., vol. 352 (2000), 5525-5561.\n\nConsider a set of ribbon tiles which are polyominoes with n squares obtained by up and right rook moves. We describe all the linear relations for the number of times each such tile can appear in a tiling of any given row convex region. We also investigate the connection with signed tilings and give applications of tileability.\n\nDownload .pdf file of the paper.\n\nCompare reviews in Zbl. Math. and Math. Reviews. See also a .pdf file of an extended abstract (10 pages), which appeared in FPSAC'98 Conference Proceedings.\n\nNote:\nConjecture 10.1 was proved by Cris Moore and IP in this paper.\nA stronger Conjecture 10.2 was later proved by Scott Sheffield in this paper.\nFor earlier literature and further reference see here.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSupport Equalities Among Ribbon Schur Functions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/319644427_Support_Equalities_Among_Ribbon_Schur_Functions)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; the following suﬃcient condition for a ribbon to have full equivalence class. ... ribbons have full equivalence class [7, Thm.\n\nending immediately before q is not Yamanouchi. Consequently, we have that v=z,\n\ncontradicting our assumption that the j th row of A(